 ## Overview
-Hardware-Specific Optimization adapts volume rendering algorithms to exploit the architectural characteristics of the target execution platform (CPU, GPU, TPU, or specialized accelerators) to maximize throughput and minimize latency. It differs from general optimization methods by directly addressing hardware constraints such as cache hierarchy, memory bandwidth, instruction-level parallelism, and execution models, rather than solely optimizing algorithmic complexity or geometric traversal costs.
+Hardware-Specific Optimization encompasses techniques that adapt volume rendering algorithms to exploit the architectural characteristics of target execution platforms (CPU, GPU, TPU, or specialized accelerators) to maximize throughput and minimize latency [S1][S2, S3, unofficial][S4][S5, unofficial]. It differs from general optimization methods by directly addressing hardware constraints such as cache hierarchy, memory bandwidth, instruction-level parallelism, and execution models, rather than solely optimizing algorithmic complexity or geometric traversal costs [S1][S2, S3, unofficial][S4][S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family build on [Ray-grid traversal](17-ray-grid-traversal.md), which advances analytically from one cell boundary to the next . The core mechanism determines the next cell by comparing distances to upcoming boundaries on the x, y, and z axes, selecting the minimum distance to advance . Optimization and cost models do not alter this fundamental geometric logic but instead optimize the *cost* of executing it or the *parameters* (like grid resolution) under which it runs.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [GPU CSG Ray Tracing with Uniform Grid Index](4098-gpu-csg-ray-tracing-with-uniform-grid-index.md)
-A GPU-based ray tracer for Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) that accelerates ray traversal by replacing unindexed full-scans of the root universe with a uniform grid index over the universe's cells. It differs from its sibling [UltraVis Cache Optimization](10355-ultravis-cache-optimization.md) and [VIZARD II](13297-vizard-ii.md) by explicitly constructing a spatial voxel structure to cull non-intersecting cells, rather than relying solely on memory hierarchy optimizations or cost-model-driven parameter tuning. It solves the performance bottleneck where rays outside the model traverse infinite CSG extensions, causing repeated full-scans of thousands of region tokens.
+A GPU-based ray tracer for Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) that accelerates ray traversal by replacing unindexed full-scans of the root universe with a uniform grid index over the universe's cells. It solves the performance bottleneck where rays outside the model traverse infinite surface extensions, causing redundant full-scans of all region tokens. Unlike its siblings, it operates as a direct f32 transcription of CPU plotting logic into a WGSL shader, maintaining exact visual parity while leveraging hardware-specific optimizations like silent voxel stepping.
 
 ### [UltraVis Cache Optimization](10355-ultravis-cache-optimization.md)
-UltraVis is a software-based volume rendering system designed to maximize cache performance on Intel Pentium III processors by addressing cache thrashing through specific memory layouts and cache partitioning. Unlike general ray-casting methods, it partitions the CPU cache into four dedicated blocks to prevent volume data from being evicted by competing structures like transfer functions or image buffers. It achieves this by combining bricked volume decomposition with a cubic interleaved address function for voxel storage, ensuring that neighboring rays accessing the same brick benefit from high cache reuse.
+UltraVis is a software-based volume rendering system designed to maximize cache performance on Intel Pentium III processors by addressing cache thrashing through specific memory layout and interleaved addressing. It sets itself apart from general ray-casting methods by enforcing a rigid cache partitioning scheme that guarantees volume data remains in a private cache block, never being swapped out by competing structures like transfer functions or image buffers. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on hardware-specific instruction sets or GPU parallelism, UltraVis focuses on CPU cache architecture constraints, using bricked decomposition and ray flocking to ensure data locality.
 
 ### [VIZARD II](13297-vizard-ii.md)
-VIZARD II is a hardware-accelerated volume rendering architecture designed to bring interactive ray casting to desktop computers by minimizing memory bandwidth requirements through quantized gradients, caching, and early ray termination. Unlike its predecessor VIZARD which used pre-shaded data, VIZARD II computes gradients on-the-fly using a quantized lookup table, and unlike the VolumePro architecture, it supports perspective rendering while relying on a table-based gradient vector lookup scheme rather than hardware gradient estimation.
+VIZARD II is a specialized hardware architecture for interactive volume rendering, developed at the University of Tübingen to bring ray casting to desktop computers. It solves the memory bandwidth bottleneck inherent in image-order ray casting by combining early ray termination, 9-bit quantized gradient look-up tables, and a specialized caching mechanism. Unlike its predecessor VIZARD, which used pre-shaded data, VIZARD II computes gradients on-the-fly via a quantized table, and unlike the VolumePro, it supports perspective rendering while using a table-based gradient scheme instead of hardware gradient estimation.
 
 ### [Hardware-Aware Hyperparameter Tuning](17622-hardware-aware-hyperparameter-tuning.md)
-Hardware-Aware Hyperparameter Tuning is an automatic process that determines optimal performance parameters (tile size, unroll factor, number of bins, batch divisor) for a specific scene and hardware configuration, ensuring correctness and performance without manual tuning. It sets itself apart from manual configuration or static heuristics by profiling the specific target views to derive exact bounds and searching the parameter space sequentially to adapt to scene geometry and hardware characteristics.
+Hardware-Aware Hyperparameter Tuning is an automatic process that determines optimal performance parameters (tile size, unroll factor, number of bins, batch divisor) for a specific scene and view set on a target hardware device, ensuring correctness and performance without manual tuning. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [GPU CSG Ray Tracing with Uniform Grid Index](4098-gpu-csg-ray-tracing-with-uniform-grid-index.md) by focusing on compile-time configuration of shader batch sizes and bin layouts rather than geometric traversal optimization or cache locality.
 
